    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-van-hove-sequence",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Van Hove sequence of bounded regions",
        "statement": "For a bounded set $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ and $R>0$, define its two-sided boundary layer\n$$\\partial_R\\Omega=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\partial\\Omega)\\leq R\\},$$\nwhere distance is Euclidean distance and $|\\cdot|$ denotes $d$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. A van Hove sequence is a sequence of bounded Lebesgue measurable sets $(\\Omega_k)_{k\\geq1}$ with $0<|\\Omega_k|<\\infty$, $|\\Omega_k|\\to\\infty$, and\n$$\\frac{|\\partial_R\\Omega_k|}{|\\Omega_k|}\\longrightarrow0\\qquad\\text{for every fixed }R>0.$$\nThe condition controls both inner and outer boundary neighborhoods. It does not impose connectedness, a bounded aspect ratio, smooth boundaries, or a periodic identification.",
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 2, 4, and 7, printed pp.172–195 / PDF pp.2–25: increasing cubes and boundary conditions. The arbitrary-shape extension below uses the explicit boundary-layer condition in the local proof."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Superstable regular pair interactions and configurational ensemble notation",
        "statement": "Assume AC. Fix an integer dimension $d\\geq1$, use the Euclidean norm $|\\cdot|_2$ from [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], define $|x|_\\infty=\\max_j|x_j|$, and use the numerical coordinate conventions in [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. A finite configuration is a finite subset $\\omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with no repeated points; its number of points is $N(\\omega)$. A pair potential is an even Borel function, measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra generated by open sets ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]), $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$, where even means $\\phi(-x)=\\phi(x)$. Define\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)=\\sum_{\\{x,y\\}\\subset\\omega}\\phi(x-y),\\qquad H_\\phi(\\varnothing)=0.$$\nThe sum is over unordered two-point subsets, so every term is evaluated at a nonzero separation; if any term is $+\\infty$, the sum is $+\\infty$.\n\nThe potential is stable if some $B\\geq0$ satisfies $H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq-BN(\\omega)$ for every finite configuration. It is superstable in the Georgii splitting convention if $\\phi=\\phi_s+\\phi_r$, where $\\phi_s$ is stable and $\\phi_r\\geq0$ everywhere and, for some $r_0,\\delta>0$, $\\phi_r(x)\\geq\\delta$ whenever $|x|_\\infty\\leq r_0$. It is lower regular if a nonnegative nonincreasing $\\psi:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,+\\infty]$ satisfies\n$$\\phi(x)\\geq-\\psi(|x|_\\infty),\\qquad \\int_0^\\infty\\psi(r)r^{d-1}\\,dr<\\infty.$$\nIt is regular if it is lower regular and, for some $R<\\infty$, $\\phi(x)\\leq\\psi(|x|_\\infty)$ whenever $|x|_\\infty\\geq R$. These are the conventions used for the interacting results on this page. A genuine divergent positive core is the separate additional condition that some nonincreasing $\\chi:(0,r_1]\\to(0,\\infty)$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ for $0<|x|_\\infty\\leq r_1$ and $\\int_0^{r_1}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=+\\infty$; it is not implied by superstability.\n\nFor $L>0$ put $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$, $V_L=L^d$, and let $H_{L,\\mathrm{free}}$ be $H_\\phi$ for configurations restricted to $\\Lambda_L$. For sufficiently large periodic cubes, periodize $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ by $\\omega^{\\mathrm{per}}=\\{x+Lk:x\\in\\omega,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\}$ and set\n$$H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}(\\omega)=\\frac12\\sum_{x\\in\\omega}\\sum_{y\\in\\omega^{\\mathrm{per}},\\ y\\ne x}\\phi(y-x),$$\ninterpreting the value as $+\\infty$ if a positive infinite term occurs. The far image sum is absolutely convergent for all sufficiently large $L$ by [[lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap]]; this is the image convention used below. With fixed numerical length and energy units, the canonical positional partition is\n$$Z(L,N;\\beta)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda_L^N}e^{-\\beta H_\\phi(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)}dq_1\\cdots dq_N,\\quad Z(L,0;\\beta)=1,$$\nwhere $\\beta>0$, and the grand partition is $\\Xi(L;a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$ for numerical activity $a>0$. The normalized unit-intensity Poisson version is $Z_Q(L;z,\\beta)=E_{Q_{\\Lambda_L}}[z^{N_{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_{L,\\mathrm{free}}}]$; since the Poisson density contributes $e^{-V_L}$, $\\Xi(L;z,\\beta)=e^{V_L}Z_Q(L;z,\\beta)$. A physical pressure identification is not part of this mathematical definition.\n\nFor a stationary probability law $P$ on the configuration space of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]], write $\\rho(P)=E_PN_{[0,1)^d}$. When the limit exists, write\n$$\\mathcal E(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}V_L^{-1}E_PH_{L,\\mathrm{free}},\\qquad I(P)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}V_L^{-1}D(P_{\\Lambda_L}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_L}).$$\nThe cited results below establish existence under their stated hypotheses; this definition does not assume it for every stationary law.",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference"
        ],
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-borel-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
