      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Periodized empirical field of a finite configuration",
        "statement": "Use the cube $\\Lambda_L=[0,L)^d$ and the translation $T_x$ defined in [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. For a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$, let $\\omega^{(L)}=\\{q+Lk:q\\in\\omega,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\}$ be its periodic continuation. Define its **periodized empirical field**\n$$R_{L,\\omega}:=\\frac1{L^d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}\\delta_{T_x\\omega^{(L)}}\\,dx.$$\nThe integral is the push-forward of normalized Lebesgue measure on $\\Lambda_L$ under $x\\mapsto T_x\\omega^{(L)}$. For a probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$, define its **empirical-field barycenter** $\\bar C_L$ by\n$$\\bar C_L(f):=\\int C_L(d\\omega)\\,R_{L,\\omega}(f)=\\frac1{L^d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}E_{C_L}[f(T_x\\omega^{(L)})]\\,dx$$\nfor local tame $f$ from [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]]. This is a spatial average over the finite box, not the raw finite-volume law viewed at a fixed origin.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference",
          "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 1, equation (1.1), printed p.172 / PDF p.2: translation-invariant empirical field formed from the periodic continuation of a finite-box configuration."
            },
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii and H. Zessin, Large deviations and the maximum entropy principle for marked point random fields",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/GiiZess.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 2–3: stationary empirical fields and their local-tame topology."
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Diagonal gap for a decreasing integrable interaction tail",
        "statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and let $\\phi$ be a regular potential with a nonnegative nonincreasing envelope $\\psi:[R_0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$, where $R_0<\\infty$ and $\\int_{R_0}^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr<\\infty$. Thus $|\\phi(x)|\\leq\\psi(|x|_\\infty)$ for $|x|_\\infty\\geq R_0$. For $g>2R_0$ set\n$$\\eta(g)=g^d\\psi(g)+\\int_g^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr.$$\nThen $\\eta(g)\\to0$ and\n$$\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)\\leq 2^{d-1}g^{-d}\\eta(g).$$\nMoreover, there is a choice $g(\\ell)>0$, for all sufficiently large real $\\ell$, such that $g(\\ell)\\to\\infty$, $g(\\ell)/\\ell\\to0$, and\n$$\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g(\\ell)}\\right)^d\\eta(g(\\ell))\\longrightarrow0.$$\nFor every fixed finite configuration and all sufficiently large periods $L$, the noncoincident periodic-image tail $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d,\\ |k|_\\infty\\geq K_0}|\\phi(x-y+Lk)|$ is absolutely convergent. The tail of the cell envelope $\\psi(|j|_\\infty-1)$ over all sufficiently large integer offsets is summable. This lemma is a deterministic tail estimate; it uses no superstability or thermodynamic-limit conclusion.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4, printed pp.181–184 / PDF pp.11–14, regularity envelopes and separated-box boundary estimates."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 1–2, printed pp.132–139 / PDF pp.5–12, summable lattice envelopes and gap estimates."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and notation above.\n\n1.1 Since $\\psi$ is nonincreasing, $0\\leq g^d\\psi(g)\\leq \\frac{d}{1-2^{-d}}\\int_{g/2}^{g}r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr$ for $g>2R_0$: on $[g/2,g]$, $\\psi(r)\\geq\\psi(g)$, and integration of $r^{d-1}$ gives the displayed coefficient. Both the interval integral and the tail integral in $\\eta(g)$ tend to zero as $g\\to\\infty$, so $\\eta(g)\\to0$. [F1, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $m\\geq2$ and $r\\in[(m-1)g,mg]$, monotonicity gives $\\psi(r)\\geq\\psi(mg)$ and $r^{d-1}\\geq((m-1)g)^{d-1}$. Hence $$ \\int_{(m-1)g}^{mg}r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr\\geq g^d(m-1)^{d-1}\\psi(mg). $$ Since $m^{d-1}\\leq2^{d-1}(m-1)^{d-1}$, summing over disjoint intervals yields $\\sum_{m\\geq2}m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)\\leq2^{d-1}g^{-d}\\int_g^\\infty r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr$. The $m=1$ term equals $g^{-d}g^d\\psi(g)$, so adding it proves the stated bound because $2^{d-1}\\geq1$. [F1, algebra]\n\n1.3 For integer $m\\geq\\lceil R_0+3\\rceil$, monotonicity gives $\\psi(r)\\geq\\psi(m-1)$ on $[m-2,m-1]$, so $\\int_{m-2}^{m-1}r^{d-1}\\psi(r)dr\\geq(m-2)^{d-1}\\psi(m-1)$. Since $m^{d-1}\\leq c_d(m-2)^{d-1}$ for these $m$, summing over disjoint unit intervals proves $\\sum_{m\\geq\\lceil R_0+3\\rceil} m^{d-1}\\psi(m-1)<\\infty$. [F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 For each integer $k\\geq1$, let $G_k$ be the least integer greater than $2R_0$ such that $\\eta(g)\\leq k^{-2d}$ for every $g\\geq G_k$; it exists by step 1.1 and the well-ordering of the positive integers. Choose a strictly increasing sequence $L_k$ with $L_k\\geq kG_k$ and $L_k\\geq k^2$. For $\\ell\\geq L_1$, let $k(\\ell)=\\max\\{k:L_k\\leq\\ell\\}$ and $g(\\ell)=\\ell/k(\\ell)$. Then $k(\\ell)\\to\\infty$, $g(\\ell)\\geq G_{k(\\ell)}$ and $g(\\ell)\\geq k(\\ell)$, while $g(\\ell)/\\ell=1/k(\\ell)$. Therefore $$ \\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g(\\ell)}\\right)^d\\eta(g(\\ell))\\leq k(\\ell)^d k(\\ell)^{-2d}=k(\\ell)^{-d}\\to0. $$ This gives all three gap limits. [F1, step 1.1, choose]\n\n3.1 Fix distinct points $x,y$ in a finite configuration and a period $L>0$. For $|k|_\\infty$ larger than $2|x-y|_\\infty/L+2R_0/L$, the triangle inequality gives $|x-y+Lk|_\\infty\\geq L|k|_\\infty/2>2R_0$. Grouping lattice indices by $m=|k|_\\infty$ and applying the sum bound of step 1.2 with gap $g=L/2$ shows $\\sum_k|\\phi(x-y+Lk)|<\\infty$ whenever $L/2>2R_0$. There are finitely many remaining images; any $+\\infty$ term makes the periodic energy $+\\infty$ by definition, while otherwise their finite sum is well-defined. This proves the periodic-image convention used in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]] for all sufficiently large $L$. [F1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-superstable-cell-energy-bound",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Cell-quadratic lower bound from superstability",
        "statement": "Let $\\phi=\\phi_s+\\phi_r$ be superstable in the splitting convention of [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]]. Suppose $H_{\\phi_s}(\\omega)\\geq-B_sN(\\omega)$ and $\\phi_r(x)\\geq\\delta>0$ for $|x|_\\infty\\leq r_0$, with $\\phi_r\\geq0$ elsewhere. For the unit cells $C_i=i+[0,1)^d$, there are constants $A>0$ and $B\\geq0$ such that every finite configuration satisfies\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq A\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C_i}(\\omega)^2-BN(\\omega).$$\nOnly finitely many summands are nonzero. This is the cell form used in the continuum pressure arguments.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 3, Lemma 3.1, printed p.178 / PDF p.8, quadratic cell-occupation coercivity from the stable-plus-repulsive decomposition."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 1, superstability definition and cell-energy condition, printed pp.131–133 / PDF pp.4–6."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The assumptions and the half-open unit-cell partition.\n\n1.1 Choose an integer $k\\geq1$ with $1/k\\leq r_0$. Partition each unit cell $C_i$ into $k^d$ half-open subcubes of side $1/k$, and denote their occupancies by $n_{i,j}$, $1\\leq j\\leq k^d$. Distinct points in one subcube have $|x-y|_\\infty<1/k\\leq r_0$, so their repulsive pair energy is at least $\\delta$. Therefore\n$$H_{\\phi_r}(\\omega)\\geq\\frac{\\delta}{2}\\sum_{i,j}n_{i,j}(n_{i,j}-1).$$\nWriting $\\bar n=N_{C_i}(\\omega)/k^d$, nonnegativity of $\\sum_j(n_{i,j}-\\bar n)^2$ expands to $\\sum_j n_{i,j}^2\\geq N_{C_i}(\\omega)^2/k^d$, and $\\sum_{i,j}n_{i,j}=N(\\omega)$. Thus $H_{\\phi_r}(\\omega)\\geq\\frac{\\delta}{2k^d}\\sum_iN_{C_i}^2-\\frac\\delta2N$. [F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 The stable part obeys $H_{\\phi_s}(\\omega)\\geq-B_sN$. Add this inequality to step 1.1 and take $A=\\delta/(2k^d)$ and $B=B_s+\\delta/2$. This proves the cell-quadratic bound. If any term is $+\\infty$, the inequality holds in the extended order; otherwise all sums are finite. [F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Stationarity and intensity of a periodized empirical field",
        "statement": "For $L>0$ and a finite configuration $\\omega\\subset\\Lambda_L$ with $N(\\omega)=N$, the measure $R_{L,\\omega}$ of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]] is a stationary probability law of finite intensity and\n$$\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=\\frac{N}{L^d}.$$\nFor any probability law $C_L$ on finite configurations in $\\Lambda_L$ with $E_{C_L}N<\\infty$, its empirical-field barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is also a stationary probability law of finite intensity and $\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}N/L^d$.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
          "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 1, equation (1.1), printed p.172 / PDF p.2: the periodic empirical field is translation invariant."
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and definitions in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 By [F1], $R_{L,\\omega}$ is the push-forward of normalized Lebesgue probability on $\\Lambda_L$, so it is a probability measure. For a bounded measurable local $f$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R^d$,\n$$R_{L,\\omega}(f\\circ T_y)=L^{-d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}f(T_{x+y}\\omega^{(L)})\\,dx.$$\nThe integrand is $L\\mathbb Z^d$-periodic in $x$. The translated cube $\\Lambda_L+y$ is another fundamental region for this lattice; partitioning it and $\\Lambda_L$ into their finitely many translated intersections and using translation invariance of Lebesgue measure gives equal integrals. Hence $R_{L,\\omega}(f\\circ T_y)=R_{L,\\omega}(f)$ for every $y$, so $R_{L,\\omega}$ is stationary. [F1, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 For bounded Borel $A$, Tonelli's theorem and the periodic continuation give\n$$R_{L,\\omega}(N_A)=L^{-d}\\int_{\\Lambda_L}\\sum_{q\\in\\omega}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}\\mathbf1_A(q+Lk+x)\\,dx.$$\nFor each fixed $q\\in\\omega$, the sets $\\Lambda_L+Lk$ partition $\\mathbb R^d$ up to null boundaries. Translating variables in each integral therefore makes the inner sum of integrals equal to $|A|$. Summing over the $N$ representatives gives $R_{L,\\omega}(N_A)=N|A|/L^d$. Taking $A=[0,1)^d$ proves $\\rho(R_{L,\\omega})=N/L^d$ and finite intensity. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 The barycenter $\\bar C_L$ is a probability mixture of the stationary laws $R_{L,\\omega}$, so it is stationary. By step 2.1 and Tonelli,\n$$\\rho(\\bar C_L)=E_{C_L}[R_{L,\\omega}(N_{[0,1)^d})]=E_{C_L}N/L^d<\\infty.$$\nThus it belongs to the finite-intensity stationary domain of $\\tau_L$. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎\n\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Superquadratic cell coercivity from a divergent or hard core",
        "statement": "Assume the potential of [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]] is stable and regular. In addition, assume either (i) $\\chi:(0,r_1]\\to(0,\\infty)$ is finite-valued and nonincreasing, $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ for $0<|x|_\\infty\\leq r_1$, and $\\int_0^{r_1}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}dr=+\\infty$; or (ii) there is a hard-core radius $r_h>0$ such that $\\phi(x)=+\\infty$ whenever $|x|_\\infty<r_h$. Then there are a nonnegative nondecreasing $h:\\mathbb N_0\\to[0,+\\infty]$ with $h(0)=0$ and $h(N)/N^2\\to+\\infty$, and a constant $b<\\infty$, such that\n$$H_\\phi(\\omega)\\geq\\sum_{i\\in\\mathbb Z^d}h(N_{C_i}(\\omega))-bN(\\omega).$$\nThe hard-core alternative permits $h(N)=+\\infty$ above the finite packing capacity of a unit cell. This is stronger than ordinary superstability and is not inferred from stability or regularity alone.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 3 and Eq. (2.26), printed pp.176–177 / PDF pp.6–7; the source's core condition is strengthened explicitly here."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 1–2, printed pp.132–139 / PDF pp.5–12, cell occupation bounds from a repulsive core and a summable lower-regular tail."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Write $N_i=N_{C_i}(\\omega)$. The negative part of each pair potential is bounded both by $2B_s$ and, at large separation, by the lower-regular envelope. Set $\\Psi_j$ to a finite constant on the finitely many near-cell offsets and to the envelope evaluated at $|j|_\\infty-1$ on the remaining offsets. The shell count $|\\{j:|j|_\\infty=m\\}|\\leq c_dm^{d-1}$ and the cell-envelope conclusion of [F2] give $\\sum_j\\Psi_j<\\infty$. Since $N_iN_j\\leq(N_i^2+N_j^2)/2$, the total negative pair contribution is at most $C\\sum_iN_i^2$ for a finite $C$. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 In case (i), choose $k_0$ with $1/k_0<r_1$. For $k\\geq k_0$, partition one unit cell into $k^d$ half-open cubes of side $1/k$, put $\\chi_k=\\chi(1/k)$, and $\\Delta\\chi_k=\\chi_k-\\chi_{k-1}\\geq0$. If the cell contains $N$ points, the identity $\\sum_jn_j^2-N=2\\sum_j\\binom{n_j}{2}$ and nonnegativity of $\\sum_j(n_j-N/k^d)^2$ give at least $\\frac12(N^2/k^d-N)$ same-subcube pairs at scale $k$. A fixed pair counted at scale $k$ has separation $s<1/k$, hence $k<1/s$; summing its nonnegative weights over all scales where it is counted is at most $\\sum_{k=k_0}^{\\lfloor1/s\\rfloor}\\Delta\\chi_k\\leq\\chi(s)$. Thus the positive core energy in the cell dominates the sum of these weighted pair counts. With $K_N=\\lfloor(N/2)^{1/d}\\rfloor$, each $k\\leq K_N$ satisfies $N^2/k^d-N\\geq N^2/(2k^d)$, so the core energy is at least $$ \\frac{N^2}{4}\\sum_{k=k_0}^{K_N}\\frac{\\Delta\\chi_k}{k^d}. $$ Summation by parts writes the coefficient as an initial constant plus $\\sum_{k=k_0}^{K_N-1}\\chi_k(k^{-d}-(k+1)^{-d})$; and $k^{-d}-(k+1)^{-d}\\geq d/(2^{d+1}k^{d+1})$. The series $\\sum_k\\chi(1/k)k^{-d-1}$ diverges: on dyadic intervals $[2^{-j-1},2^{-j}]$, monotonicity bounds the integral above by $C_d2^{-jd}\\chi(2^{-j-1})$, while the corresponding block $2^{j+1}\\leq k<2^{j+2}$ contributes at least $c_d2^{-jd}\\chi(2^{-j-1})$ to that series. The divergent core integral therefore forces the displayed coefficient to tend to infinity. [F1, algebra, construct]\n\n2.1 In case (i), sum the cell core bound from step 1.2 and subtract the negative-pair bound from step 1.1. The resulting coefficient of $N_i^2$ tends to infinity with $N_i$. Above a finite threshold it dominates $2CN_i^2$; below that threshold its possible negative remainder is bounded by a constant times $N_i$. Taking $h(N)$ to be half the positive cell lower bound above the threshold and zero below it gives a nondecreasing extended-valued function with $h(N)/N^2\\to\\infty$ and $H_\\phi\\geq\\sum_i h(N_i)-bN$. [F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 In case (ii), choose $k$ with $1/k<r_h$ and partition every unit cell into $k^d$ subcubes. A finite-energy configuration has at most one point in each subcube, so $N_i\\leq k^d$ for every $i$. Define $h(N)=0$ for $N\\leq k^d$ and $h(N)=+\\infty$ for $N>k^d$. If any cell exceeds this occupancy, the configuration has a forbidden pair and $H_\\phi=+\\infty$; otherwise stability gives $H_\\phi\\geq-BN=\\sum_i h(N_i)-BN$. This proves the hard-core alternative. [F1, algebra, construct] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Canonical free-cube limit at every interior attainable density",
        "statement": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], fix $\\beta>0$, and define $Z(L,N;\\beta)$ as in that definition. For sequences $L_j\\to\\infty$ and integers $N_j\\geq0$ with $N_j/L_j^d\\to\\rho$, define\n$$b(\\rho)=\\sup\\left\\{\\limsup_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta):\\frac{N_j}{L_j^d}\\to\\rho\\right\\}.$$\nLet $F$ be the concave envelope of $b$ on $[0,\\infty)$, namely the supremum of $\\sum_{i=1}^m\\theta_i b(\\rho_i)$ over finite lists with $\\theta_i\\geq0$, $\\sum_i\\theta_i=1$, $\\rho_i\\geq0$, and $\\sum_i\\theta_i\\rho_i=\\rho$; the empty supremum is $-\\infty$. On the interior of $\\{\\rho:F(\\rho)>-\\infty\\}$, $F$ is finite and continuous. For every $\\rho>0$ in that interior and every such sequence $(L_j,N_j)$,\n$$\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}L_j^{-d}\\log Z(L_j,N_j;\\beta)=F(\\rho).$$\nNo claim is made at a close-packing endpoint or at any other excluded endpoint.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-exponential-addition-formula": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-natural-logarithm-laws": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 4 and Lemma 7.1, printed pp.177 and 192 / PDF pp.7 and 22: attainable densities and ensemble limits. The fixed-N extension is proved locally."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 0–3, printed pp.127–145 / PDF pp.1–18: stability, lower regularity, block packing and pressure conventions."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Dereudre, Introduction to the theory of Gibbs point processes",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.08105",
              "locator": "Section 1.3, PDF pp.10–12: finite-volume canonical and grand-canonical point-process normalization; its limit theorems assume finite range."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Stability and [F3] give $Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq e^{\\beta BN}L^{dN}/N!$. If $N/L^d\\to\\rho>0$, the factorial integral bounds [F4] imply $$ \\limsup L^{-d}\\log Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq \\rho(1+\\beta B-\\log\\rho), $$ so $b(\\rho)$ and $F(\\rho)$ are bounded above on every compact positive-density interval. The vacuum term gives $b(0)=0$: $Z(L,0)=1$ is a lower bound, and the same stability estimate with $N/L^d\\to0$ gives a nonpositive upper limit. [F1, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n1.2 The effective domain of $F$ has nonempty interior. Fix a core cube of side $s>0$ and a gap $g>R_0$; place one particle in each selected core of a lattice of spacing $s+g$ inside a large box. The absolute interaction from any one occupied core to all others is bounded by $C\\sum_{m\\geq1}m^{d-1}\\psi(mg)<\\infty$ by [F1]–[F2]. Restricting each particle to a subcube of volume $v_0>0$ therefore gives $Z(L,N;\\beta)\\geq v_0^N e^{-C_0N}$ for every density strictly below $(s+g)^{-d}$, after omitting unused lattice cells. Thus $F$ is finite on a nonempty interval of positive densities; by its definition as a finite-mixture envelope it is concave. [F1, F2, F3, algebra, construct]\n\n2.1 Fix a finite list of core preparations $(s_i,n_i)$ with $0<Z(s_i,n_i;\\beta)<\\infty$. For each $i$, choose a gap $g_i=g(s_i)$ from [F2] along a sequence $s_i\\to\\infty$ so that $g_i/s_i\\to0$ and the positive interaction between distinct cores in the same lattice packing, divided by core volume, tends to zero. Set tile side $a_i=s_i+g_i$ and effective density $r_i=n_i/a_i^d$. If $M_i$ such tiles carry exactly $n_i$ particles in their central $s_i$-cubes, the same shell count as in the proof of [[thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure]] gives cross-energy penalty per occupied volume at most $C (n_i/s_i^d)^2(s_i/g_i)^d\\eta(g_i)$, which tends to zero by [F2]. Exact factorization over disjoint core cubes follows because assigning the labeled coordinates to their cores contributes $N!/\\prod_i(n_i!)^{M_i}$, cancelling the global $N!$. [F2, F3, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 For any finite convex combination in the definition of $F(\\rho)$, choose core preparations with densities tending to its $\\rho_i$ and rates tending to $b(\\rho_i)$. If their effective densities do not strictly bracket the target $\\rho$, add arbitrarily small weights at one attainable density below and one above $\\rho$; such densities exist because $\\rho$ is interior. Their mixture coefficients can then be perturbed by quantities tending to zero so the effective mean is exactly $\\rho$. Place the fixed core types in finitely many macroscopic slabs with these volume fractions, inserting fixed empty layers wider than $R_0$ between slabs. For fixed core sizes and gaps, the distinct-slab cross energy divided by total volume tends to zero: for interaction distances at most $R$, only $O(RL^{d-1})$ particles lie in the interface strips, while the remaining contribution is bounded by a constant times $L^d\\sum_{|j|_\\infty>R}\\Psi_j$; take $L\\to\\infty$ and then $R\\to\\infty$. [F1, F2, step 2.1, construct]\n\n4.1 First reserve an outer layer of fixed width $W$ and adjust slab fractions in the remaining volume to match the target density; the strict bracketing in step 3.1 absorbs the reserved layer's $O(WL^{d-1})$ volume without assuming its one-particle capacity replaces a bulk density. Rounding the finitely many core counts then changes particle number by at most $C_1L^{d-1}$ with $C_1$ depending on the fixed preparations, not on $W$, because only boundary tiles are incomplete. Choose $W$ large enough to contain at least $C_1L^{d-1}$ small cells separated from each other and every core by more than $R_0$. Put each missing particle in one such cell. Its interaction with the fixed preparations is bounded by the same summable tail, so the correction changes log weight by only $O(L^{d-1})$. This constructs exactly $N_L$ particles for every sequence $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho$, and gives $$ \\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Z(L,N_L;\\beta)\\geq\\sum_i\\theta_i\\frac{\\log Z(s_i,n_i;\\beta)-\\mathrm{cross}_i}{a_i^d}, $$ where $\\mathrm{cross}_i/a_i^d\\to0$ by step 2.1. Taking preparations approaching the selected convex combination and then the supremum gives $\\liminf\\geq F(\\rho)$. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, step 3.1, construct]\n\n5.1 By the definition of $b$, every sequence with density tending $\\rho$ has limsup rate at most $b(\\rho)\\leq F(\\rho)$. Together with step 4.1 this proves the asserted limit. A finite concave function on an open interval is continuous: fix $a<\\rho<b$ in the interval; the chord slopes from $a$ and $b$ bound the difference quotients on a smaller interval around $\\rho$, yielding a finite local Lipschitz bound. This establishes finiteness and continuity of $F$ on its effective interior while leaving excluded endpoints undecided. [F4, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 4.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Free-cube grand-pressure limit for decreasing integrable tails",
        "statement": "Let $\\phi$ be stable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and fix $a,\\beta>0$. For free-boundary cubes define $\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}a^NZ(L,N;\\beta)$. Then\n$$1\\leq\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)\\leq\\exp(ae^{\\beta B}L^d),\\qquad p(a,\\beta):=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(a,\\beta)$$\nexists and is finite. This theorem assumes only stability and the decreasing integrable two-sided tail in regularity; it does not assert canonical fixed-density convergence, periodic-boundary equality, or arbitrary-region limits.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "def-nonnegative-extended-series"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "prop-lebesgue-measure-is-sigma-finite-and-finite-on-bounded-sets": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-exponential-addition-formula": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-nonnegative-extended-series": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4, printed pp.181–184 / PDF pp.11–14, free-cube pressure comparison and boundary terms."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 1–3, printed pp.131–145 / PDF pp.4–18, stable grand normalization, lattice-shell estimates and thermodynamic pressure."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Dereudre, Introduction to the theory of Gibbs point processes",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.08105",
              "locator": "Section 1.3, PDF pp.10–12, finite-volume grand-canonical normalization; its infinite-volume variational principle is restricted to finite-range interactions."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Put $q=ae^{\\beta B}$. For every $N$-point configuration, $0\\leq e^{-\\beta H_\\phi}\\leq e^{\\beta BN}$ by [A1]. Applying [F3] to the finite-volume integral gives $Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq(qL^d)^N/N!$. Summing by [F4] yields $\\Xi_L\\leq e^{qL^d}$; the empty configuration contributes exactly $1$, so $\\Xi_L\\geq1$. [A1, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n2.1 Choose $K>eq+1$ and an integer cutoff $n_\\ell$ with $K\\ell^d<n_\\ell\\leq K\\ell^d+1$. For $N>n_\\ell$, $1\\leq e^{N-K\\ell^d}$, so $$ \\sum_{N>n_\\ell}Z(\\ell,N;\\beta)\\leq e^{-K\\ell^d}\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\frac{(eq\\ell^d)^N}{N!}=e^{(eq-K)\\ell^d}\\leq e^{-\\ell^d}. $$ Because $\\Xi_\\ell\\geq1$, the truncated sum $\\Xi_\\ell^{\\rm cut}=\\sum_{N=0}^{n_\\ell}Z(\\ell,N;\\beta)$ satisfies $\\Xi_\\ell^{\\rm cut}\\geq(1-e^{-\\ell^d})\\Xi_\\ell$. [A1, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Fix a core side $\\ell$ and a gap $g>2R_0$. Place $M=\\lfloor L/(\\ell+g)\\rfloor^d$ disjoint core cubes of side $\\ell$ in $[0,L)^d$ on the lattice of spacing $\\ell+g$. Restrict every core to at most $n_\\ell$ particles. If two core indices differ by maximum norm $m\\geq1$, every pair between them has separation at least $mg$ in maximum norm. The number of offsets in shell $m$ is $(2m+1)^d-(2m-1)^d\\leq c_dm^{d-1}$. By [F1], the sum of positive cross-core pair energies is therefore at most $$ D_{L,\\ell,g}\\leq c_dM n_\\ell^2\\sum_{m\\geq1}m^{d-1}\\psi(mg). $$ Negative cross-core energies only increase the Boltzmann weight. Splitting the grand sum by the particle counts in these disjoint cores, Tonelli [F5] and the multinomial coefficient show that the global $N!$ cancels the assignments of labeled coordinates to cores. Thus $$ \\Xi_L\\geq e^{-\\beta D_{L,\\ell,g}}(\\Xi_\\ell^{\\rm cut})^M. $$ [F1, F3, F5, step 2.1, construct]\n\n4.1 Divide the last inequality by $L^d$ and let $L\\to\\infty$ with $\\ell,g$ fixed. Since $M/L^d\\to(\\ell+g)^{-d}$, [F2] gives $$ \\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L\\geq\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{\\ell+g}\\right)^d\\left[\\ell^{-d}\\log\\Xi_\\ell+\\ell^{-d}\\log(1-e^{-\\ell^d})-C_dK_1^2\\left(\\frac{\\ell}{g}\\right)^d\\eta(g)\\right], $$ where $K_1=K+1$ bounds $n_\\ell/\\ell^d$ for $\\ell\\geq1$ and $C_d$ absorbs the fixed shell-count constant and $\\beta$. [F2, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 The numbers $p_\\ell=\\ell^{-d}\\log\\Xi_\\ell$ lie in $[0,q]$ by step 1.1, so choose $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$ with $p_{\\ell_j}\\to\\limsup_{\\ell\\to\\infty}p_\\ell$. Set $g=g(\\ell_j)$ from [F2]. Then $g/\\ell_j\\to0$, the volume fraction tends to $1$, the cutoff logarithm divided by $\\ell_j^d$ tends to zero, and the cross-core error tends to zero. Step 4.1 therefore yields $\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}p_L\\geq\\limsup_{L\\to\\infty}p_L$. The reverse inequality is automatic for every real sequence, so the limit exists; step 1.1 makes it finite. [F2, step 1.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-canonical-grand-duality",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Canonical–grand-canonical thermodynamic duality",
        "statement": "Let $F_\\beta$ be the finite concave canonical rate on the interior attainable-density interval from [[thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit]]. Extend it by $F_\\beta(0)=0$, by its interior upper-semicontinuous limit at any finite upper endpoint of its effective domain, and by $-\\infty$ outside the closed effective domain; retain the value $-\\infty$ if the endpoint limit is $-\\infty$. For $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$P(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(e^\\alpha,\\beta),$$\nwhose existence is [[thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure]]. Then\n$$P(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\sup_{\\rho\\geq0}\\bigl(F_\\beta(\\rho)+\\alpha\\rho\\bigr),\\qquad F_\\beta(\\rho)=\\inf_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R}\\bigl(P(\\alpha,\\beta)-\\alpha\\rho\\bigr)$$\nfor every $\\rho$ in the closed effective domain of $F_\\beta$. These are thermodynamic limits of positional partition functions. They do not assert local-observable equivalence, a finite-volume endpoint realization, or a thin energy-shell limit.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit",
          "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
