        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Fix an interior attainable density $\\rho>0$ and a sequence $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho$. The grand sum contains the nonnegative term $e^{\\alpha N_L}Z(L,N_L;\\beta)$, so $\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log\\Xi_L(e^\\alpha,\\beta)\\geq F_\\beta(\\rho)+\\alpha\\rho$ by [F1]. Taking the supremum over interior densities gives the lower bound. The vacuum term $Z(L,0)=1$ gives the value $0$ at density zero. Endpoint values are limits from the interior by their definition, so they do not enlarge the supremum. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Put $q=e^{\\alpha+\\beta B}$. For each $N\\geq0$, stability and the box-volume formula give $e^{\\alpha N}Z(L,N;\\beta)\\leq(qL^d)^N/N!$. Choose $K>eq+1$. For $N>KL^d$, $1\\leq e^{N-KL^d}$, so the factorial bound gives $\\sum_{N>KL^d}e^{\\alpha N}Z(L,N;\\beta) \\leq e^{-KL^d}\\sum_{N\\geq0}\\frac{(eqL^d)^N}{N!} \\leq e^{-L^d}.$ The remaining at most $KL^d+2$ terms are bounded by their maximum times this number; its logarithm divided by $L^d$ tends to zero. [A1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Choose maximizing retained particle numbers and a subsequence with $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho\\in[0,K]$; nested-interval bisection on the compact interval $[0,K]$ supplies the subsequence. The canonical upper rate is at most $b(\\rho)\\leq F_\\beta(\\rho)$, since the one-term mixture in the definition of $F_\\beta$ includes $b(\\rho)$. At $\\rho=0$, stability gives upper rate at most $0=F_\\beta(0)$. If $u$ is a finite upper endpoint and $b(u)>-\\infty$, mix a preparation approaching $b(u)$ with an interior preparation of density $r<u$, in slab fractions $1-\\epsilon$ and $\\epsilon$. The exact packing inequality in [F1] gives $F_\\beta((1-\\epsilon)u+\\epsilon r)\\geq(1-\\epsilon)b(u)+\\epsilon b(r).$ As $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, the left side tends to the assigned interior endpoint limit; hence $b(u)\\leq F_\\beta(u)$. If $b(u)=-\\infty$ this bound is automatic. Outside the closed effective domain, $b(\\rho)=-\\infty$, again because a finite $b(\\rho)$ would make $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\geq b(\\rho)$. Thus the maximum retained term has limiting rate at most $\\sup_{\\rho\\geq0}(F_\\beta(\\rho)+\\alpha\\rho)$. The tail estimate in step 1.2 and the lower bound in step 1.1 prove the first equality. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 The extension $F_\\beta$ is proper, upper-semicontinuous and concave. It is finite and concave on its open effective interior by [F1], hence continuous there. At zero, stability and the concavity of $g(\\rho)=\\rho(1+\\beta B-\\log\\rho)$ give $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\leq g(\\rho)\\to0$. The sparse separated-cell lower bound in [F1] gives $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\geq c\\rho$ for a fixed finite $c$, so $F_\\beta(\\rho)\\to0=F_\\beta(0)$. At any finite upper endpoint use the assigned interior upper-semicontinuous limit; outside the closed effective domain set $F_\\beta=-\\infty$. Hence $G=-F_\\beta$, with $G=+\\infty$ on negative densities, is a proper closed convex extended-real function on $\\mathbb R$. Its conjugate is $G^*(\\alpha)=\\sup_{\\rho\\geq0}\\{\\alpha\\rho+F_\\beta(\\rho)\\}=P(\\alpha,\\beta)$ by steps 1.1–2.2. Applying [F3] gives $G(\\rho)=\\sup_\\alpha\\{\\alpha\\rho-P(\\alpha,\\beta)\\}$, which is the second equality. The endpoint convention is needed for closedness; it asserts no finite-volume endpoint realization. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-superstable-van-hove-pressure",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Free-wall pressure on arbitrary van Hove shapes under superstability",
        "statement": "Assume a superstable regular pair potential $\\phi$ as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and the van Hove definition [[def-tdmath-van-hove-sequence]]. For a bounded Lebesgue-measurable $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, define $\\mathcal S_N(\\Lambda)=\\{(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)\\in\\Lambda^N:q_i\\neq q_j\\ (i\\neq j)\\}$ and $H_N(q)=\\sum_{1\\leq i<j\\leq N}\\phi(q_i-q_j)$. With $e^{-\\beta(+\\infty)}=0$, set\n$$Z_\\Lambda(N;\\beta)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\mathcal S_N(\\Lambda)}e^{-\\beta H_N(q)}\\,dq_1\\cdots dq_N,\\qquad Z_\\Lambda(0;\\beta)=1,\\qquad \\Xi_\\Lambda(z,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}z^NZ_\\Lambda(N;\\beta).$$\nThis is the labelled positional Lebesgue integral with indistinguishable-particle normalization and includes no exterior interaction. For $\\Lambda=[0,L)^d$ it agrees with $Z(L,N;\\beta)$ and $\\Xi(L;z,\\beta)$ from [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]]. For every van Hove sequence $(\\Omega_k)$ and fixed $z,\\beta>0$, the free-wall grand pressure satisfies\n$$\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac1{|\\Omega_k|}\\log\\Xi_{\\Omega_k}(z,\\beta)=p(z,\\beta),$$\nwhere $p(z,\\beta)$ is the free-cube limit from [[thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure]]. The extra superstability hypothesis supplies the uniform cell-occupation bound used below; no arbitrary-shape theorem under stability alone is claimed.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "def-tdmath-van-hove-sequence",
          "lem-tdmath-superstable-cell-energy-bound",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-van-hove-sequence": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-superstable-cell-energy-bound": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Proposition 2.6, printed pp.140–141 / PDF pp.14–15: uniform correlation-density bound for finite-volume superstable Gibbs laws; the original proof was visually inspected."
            },
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 4, printed pp.181–184 / PDF pp.11–14: boundary comparison and shape-independent pressure method."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 First take a finite union $\\mathcal R$ of unit cells and its free Gibbs law. By [L1] and Tonelli [F5],\n$$E N_i^2=E N_i+E[N_i(N_i-1)] \\leq \\xi |C_i\\cap\\mathcal R|+\\xi^2|C_i\\cap\\mathcal R|^2 \\leq M:=\\xi+\\xi^2.$$\nLet the cells be partitioned into boxes of side $\\ell$. For each cell $i$, let $e_i$ be the sum of the lower-regular lattice envelope over cells $j$ in other boxes. The negative cross-box energy obeys $W^-\\leq\\tfrac12\\sum_i e_iN_i^2$, because $N_iN_j\\leq(N_i^2+N_j^2)/2$. Therefore $E W^-\\leq(M/2)\\sum_i e_i$. If $\\sum_i e_i>0$, then $P(W^->M\\sum_i e_i)\\leq E W^-/(M\\sum_i e_i)\\leq1/2$; if the sum is zero, $W^-=0$ almost surely. Thus the event $W^-\\leq M\\sum_i e_i$ has probability at least $1/2$. [F1, F3, F5, L1, algebra]\n\n2.1 For each $\\Omega_k$, let $\\mathcal R_k$ be the union of unit cells meeting $\\Omega_k$. Then $\\Xi_{\\Omega_k}\\leq\\Xi_{\\mathcal R_k}$ by domain monotonicity, and $|\\mathcal R_k|/|\\Omega_k|\\to1$ by the van Hove boundary condition. Partition $\\mathcal R_k$ into boxes of $\\ell$ unit cells per side. The probability event from step 1.1 has full Gibbs probability at least $1/2$; on it, $H_{\\mathrm{total}}\\geq H_{\\mathrm{in}}-M\\sum_i e_i$, where $H_{\\mathrm{in}}$ is the sum of within-box energies. Therefore\n$$\\Xi_{\\mathcal R_k}/2\\leq e^{\\beta M\\sum_i e_i}\\prod_B\\Xi_{\\mathcal R_k\\cap B}.$$\nIndeed, $\\Xi_{\\mathcal R_k}\\mu_{\\mathcal R_k}(E)=\\int_Ee^{-\\beta H_{\\mathrm{total}}}d\\Pi\\leq e^{\\beta M\\sum_i e_i}\\int_Ee^{-\\beta H_{\\mathrm{in}}}d\\Pi$, and the last integral is bounded by the unrestricted product over boxes. Each partial-box partition is at most the full $\\ell$-cube partition. For one full box, $E_\\ell=\\sum_{i\\in B,j\\notin B}\\Psi(|i-j|)$ satisfies $E_\\ell/\\ell^d\\to0$: cells within fixed distance $R$ of the boundary are $O_R(\\ell^{d-1})$; all other cells contribute at most the summable tail beyond $R$. Thus\n$$\\limsup_k|\\Omega_k|^{-1}\\log\\Xi_{\\Omega_k} \\leq\\ell^{-d}\\bigl[\\log\\Xi_{\\Lambda_\\ell}+\\beta M E_\\ell\\bigr].$$\nLet $\\ell\\to\\infty$ and use [F4]. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, L1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For the lower bound, fix core side $\\ell$ and gap $g$, and retain every whole $(\\ell+g)$-cell whose core lies in $\\Omega_k$. By the van Hove property [F2], the retained count divided by $|\\Omega_k|$ tends $(\\ell+g)^{-d}$. Restrict each core to at most $K\\ell^d$ particles. Exact $N!$ cancellation factorizes their grand weights. The lattice-shell count and [F1] bound positive cross energy per core by $C K^2(\\ell/g)^d\\eta(g)$, with $\\eta(g)\\to0$ from the integrable tail. This gives the same separated-core lower bound as the free-cube packing proof. First let $k\\to\\infty$, then choose $g=g(\\ell)$ from [[lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap]] and let $\\ell\\to\\infty$; packing fraction tends one and the cross penalty vanishes. The resulting liminf is at least the cube pressure [F4]. [F1, F2, F4, step 2.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-uniform-tempered-exterior-pressure",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Uniform exterior-pressure limit for tempered configurations",
        "statement": "Assume a stable regular pair potential $\\phi$ as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], with stability constant $B$, and the genuine finite-valued positive-core condition of [[lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core]]: for some $r_0>0$, a nonincreasing $\\chi:(0,r_0]\\to(0,\\infty)$ satisfies $\\phi(x)\\geq\\chi(|x|_\\infty)$ for $0<|x|_\\infty\\leq r_0$ and $\\int_0^{r_0}\\chi(r)r^{d-1}\\,dr=+\\infty$. Let $\\Lambda_n=[-n-\\tfrac12,n+\\tfrac12)^d$, $V_n=(2n+1)^d$, $C_i=i+[-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)^d$, and $\\mathcal C_{\\rm lf}$ be the set of locally finite simple configurations $\\zeta\\subset\\mathbb R^d$. For $t\\geq0$ define\n$$\\Omega(t)=\\left\\{\\zeta\\in\\mathcal C_{\\rm lf}:\\sum_{i\\in\\Lambda_n\\cap\\mathbb Z^d}N_{C_i}(\\zeta)^2\\leq tV_n\\text{ for every integer }n\\geq0\\right\\}.$$\nFor $\\omega=(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)\\in\\mathcal S_N(\\Lambda_n)$, write $H_{n,\\mathrm{free}}(\\omega)=\\sum_{1\\leq i<j\\leq N}\\phi(q_i-q_j)$. For $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$, let $\\zeta_n^{\\rm out}=\\zeta\\setminus\\Lambda_n$ and define the finite-volume boundary Hamiltonian\n$$H_{n,\\zeta}(\\omega)=H_{n,\\mathrm{free}}(\\omega)+\\sum_{i=1}^N\\sum_{y\\in\\zeta_n^{\\rm out}}\\phi(q_i-y).$$\nThe exterior series has finite negative part by the proof below; its extended value may be $+\\infty$, with $e^{-\\beta(+\\infty)}=0$. Define\n$$Z_{n,\\zeta}(N;\\beta)=\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\mathcal S_N(\\Lambda_n)}e^{-\\beta H_{n,\\zeta}(\\omega)}\\,dq_1\\cdots dq_N,\\qquad Z_{n,\\zeta}(0;\\beta)=1,\\qquad \\Xi_{n,\\zeta}(z,\\beta)=\\sum_{N\\geq0}z^NZ_{n,\\zeta}(N;\\beta).$$\nFor fixed $z,\\beta>0$ and $t<\\infty$, the free-wall grand pressure with this exterior pair energy satisfies\n$$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sup_{\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)}\\left|\\frac1{V_n}\\log\\Xi_{n,\\zeta}(z,\\beta)-p(z,\\beta)\\right|=0,$$\nwhere $p(z,\\beta)$ is the free-cube pressure from [[thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure]]. The exterior configuration is restricted to the stated fixed square-count class; no volume-filling external field is included. The conclusion is conditional on the genuine positive core and does not infer it from superstability alone.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure",
          "thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-superquadratic-positive-core": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-decreasing-integrable-tail-free-cube-pressure": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 6, printed pp.188–191 / PDF pp.18–21: tempered exterior pressure comparison and near/far interactions."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Propositions 2.5–2.7 and 5.2–5.3, printed pp.140–144 and 151–155 / PDF pp.14–18 and 25–29: correlation and tempered moment bounds. The complete probability proof was visually checked."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Write $T_n=\\sum_{i\\subset\\Lambda_n}N_i^2$ and $V_n=|\\Lambda_n|$. From [F3], $T_n$ is controlled by $H_n+bN_n$ and the superquadratic penalty dominates $N_i^2$ for large cell counts. For exterior cells at distance at least $k$, the lattice tail gives an energy lower error at most $\\delta_k(T_n+c_tV_n)$, where $\\delta_k\\to0$. For the finitely many near exterior cells, use $mN\\leq g(N)+g^*(m)$ with $g(N)=N\\sqrt{h(N)}$. Since $g(N)/N^2\\to\\infty$, its Legendre transform satisfies $g^*(m)/m^2\\to0$: for each $\\epsilon>0$, $g(N)\\geq N^2/(4\\epsilon)-C_\\epsilon$, so $g^*(m)\\leq\\epsilon m^2+C_\\epsilon$. The exterior square-count bound then makes the near-cell contribution at most $\\epsilon T_n+\\epsilon V_n+O(n^{d-1})$, uniformly over $\\zeta\\in\\Omega(t)$. [F1, F2, F3, L1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Combining near and far estimates, for each $\\epsilon>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$, the cross energy obeys $W(\\omega,\\zeta\\setminus\\Lambda_n)\\geq-\\epsilon T_n-\\epsilon V_n$, uniformly in $\\zeta$. Use $T_n\\leq A_\\epsilon(H_n+bN_n)+C_\\epsilon V_n$ from [F3], and absorb the resulting linear-in-$N_n$ term into activity. This gives\n$$\\Xi_{n,\\zeta}(\\beta,z)\\leq e^{c\\epsilon V_n}\\Xi_{n,\\mathrm{free}}(\\beta(1-c\\epsilon),ze^{c\\epsilon}),$$\nfor a fixed constant $c$ depending only on the coercivity constants. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 For the lower bound, restrict interior particles to a smaller cube with an empty corridor of width $k$. All exterior pairs are then in the far tail, and the same shell estimate bounds the absolute cross energy by $\\epsilon T_{n-k}+\\epsilon V_n$. The cell penalty absorbs the count term and yields\n$$\\Xi_{n,\\zeta}(\\beta,z)\\geq e^{-c\\epsilon V_n}\\Xi_{n-k,\\mathrm{free}}(\\beta(1+c\\epsilon),ze^{-c\\epsilon})$$\nuniformly over $\\zeta$. The removed corridor volume fraction vanishes for fixed $k$. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Divide steps 2.1–2.2 by $V_n$ and take $n\\to\\infty$; [F4] supplies the perturbed cube pressure limits. These limits are finite convex functions of $(\\beta,\\log z)$, by Hölder’s inequality [F5], since finite-volume log partition functions are convex and pointwise limits preserve convexity. A finite convex function on an open parameter rectangle is continuous, by bounding coordinate secant slopes on a smaller rectangle. Let $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ in both bounds. The upper and lower limits coincide with the free-cube pressure, uniformly over the entire exterior class. [F4, F5, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Superstable regular continuum grand pressure and variational formula",
        "statement": "Assume AC, the ultrafilter extension principle, DC, and HB. Let $\\phi$ be superstable and regular as in [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]], and fix $z,\\beta>0$. For stationary finite-intensity configuration laws, let $I(P)$ be relative entropy density with respect to the independent unit-intensity Poisson law, $\\rho(P)=E_PN_{[0,1)^d}$, and $\\Phi(P)$ the limiting positional energy density. The existence and lower semicontinuity of $\\Phi$ under these hypotheses are part of the proof below. For free and periodic cubes define\n$$Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}=E_{Q_{\\Lambda_L}}\\!\\left[z^{N_{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_{L,bc}}\\right],\\qquad bc\\in\\{\\mathrm{free},\\mathrm{per}\\}.$$\nThen the limit $p_Q(z,\\beta)=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}L^{-d}\\log Z_{L,z,\\beta,bc}$ exists, is independent of $bc$, and\n$$p_Q(z,\\beta)=-\\min_{P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}}\\{I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z\\}.$$\nThe minimizer set is nonempty and compact in the local-tame topology $\\tau_L$. This theorem concerns positional configurations only. If $\\Xi_{L,z,\\beta}$ is instead normalized against Lebesgue measure with $1/N!$, then $\\Xi_{L,z,\\beta}=e^{L^d}Z_{L,z,\\beta,\\mathrm{free}}$, so its logarithmic rate is $p_Q+1$.",
