        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 4 and Sections 5–7, printed pp.177–195 / PDF pp.7–25: density interval with upper energy event, entropy minimizers and finite multiplier."
            },
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii and H. Zessin, Large deviations and the maximum entropy principle for marked point random fields",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/GiiZess.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 3 and 5, printed pp.188–195 and 197–204: entropy upper/lower event estimates and conditional laws."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The hypotheses and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Define $m(\\rho,e)=\\inf\\{I(P):\\rho(P)=\\rho,\\Phi(P)\\leq e\\}$ and $s=-m$. For stationary finite-entropy laws $P,R$, the binary-mixture chain rule on a bounded cube $G$ gives $\\theta D(P_G\\Vert Q_G)+(1-\\theta)D(R_G\\Vert Q_G)=D((\\theta P+(1-\\theta)R)_G\\Vert Q_G)+I(S;X_G)$, with $0\\leq I(S;X_G)\\leq H(S)<\\infty$. Divide by $|G|$ and use [F4]; the label entropy vanishes, so $I$ is affine on finite mixtures. The expectation $\\rho$ and finite-volume energy expectations are affine, hence their limits $\\rho,\\Phi$ are affine where finite. Mixtures of feasible laws show $m$ is convex and $s$ concave. If $(\\rho_n,e_n)\\to(\\rho,e)$ and $\\liminf m(\\rho_n,e_n)<\\infty$, choose near-minimizers with bounded $I$. Compactness [F2] gives a convergent subsequence; continuity of $\\rho$ by [F6] and lower semicontinuity of $\\Phi,I$ in [F1] show the limit is feasible with entropy no greater than the liminf. Thus $m$ is lower semicontinuous and $s$ upper semicontinuous; the same compactness argument attains every finite infimum. For strict feasibility, choose $\\rho_1$ in the interior of $D$ below $\\nu(\\phi)$ with a finite-energy, finite-entropy law $R$. The inequality $e>\\inf_{\\rho\\in D}e_0(\\rho)$ gives $\\rho_2\\in D$ and a finite-entropy law $P$ with $\\Phi(P)<e$. If $\\rho_2$ is at an endpoint of $D$, mix $P$ with a sufficiently small positive amount of $R$; affinity of $\\rho,\\Phi,I$ keeps the energy below $e$ and moves the density into the interior of $D$. [F1, F2, F4, F5, F6, algebra]\n\n2.1 For the lower event bound, take a feasible stationary law with strict density and energy inequalities. Approximate it by separated independent blocks: condition each block on a finite-energy, bounded-count event of probability tending to one, separate blocks by a fixed empty corridor, and average the phase. Its entropy per volume tends to $I(P)$; its empirical density and energy converge to $\\rho(P)$ and $\\Phi(P)$. The spatial likelihood theorem gives Poisson probability at least $\\exp[-L^d(I(P)+o(1))]$ for the joint typical event, which lies in $A_L$. If a minimizing law lies on a constraint boundary, mix it with the strict feasible law. The mixture remains feasible for sufficiently small mixing weight and its entropy tends to the boundary law's entropy by the entropy affinity established in step 1.1 and lower semicontinuity. Thus the open strict event and the closed event have the same entropy infimum. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For the upper event bound, let $G_L=Q_{\\Lambda_L}(\\cdot\\mid A_L)$. Its exact entropy is $D(G_L\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_L})=-\\log Q_{\\Lambda_L}(A_L)$. Form the auxiliary stationary law by placing independent copies of $G_L$ in disjoint blocks, leaving a fixed empty corridor, and averaging the block phase. The independent-increment Poisson entropy chain rule bounds this auxiliary law's specific entropy by the displayed finite-volume entropy per volume. Its local limits preserve the closed density and energy constraints by lower semicontinuity of $I$ and $\\Phi$, giving the rate upper bound by $m$. The matching lower bound follows from step 2.1. This auxiliary block law is used only for the event exponent. [F1, F2, F5, F6, L1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 Let $P$ be a constrained entropy minimizer. Georgii's Theorem 4 [L1] applies with $e_0(\\rho)=\\inf\\{\\Phi(P):\\rho(P)=\\rho,\\ I(P)<\\infty\\}$ and $\\nu(\\phi)$ the same finite-entropy, finite-energy density supremum. Its Section 7 proof separates the strict-feasibility constraint set in finite dimension [F3]; the strict feasible law from step 1.1 makes the objective multiplier nonzero, so normalization yields $\\beta>0$ and finite $\\alpha=\\log z$ with $P$ minimizing $I+\\beta\\Phi-\\alpha\\rho$. This is the exact source conclusion, not an assumed exposed density. The grand variational principle [F1] identifies these constrained minimizers with grand pressure minimizers. By [L1], the conditional and grand empirical fields concentrate on their common minimizer set; the fields are the stationary laws in [F7]. If the minimizer is unique, both converge to it in probability in $\\tau_L$. [F1, F3, F7, L1, step 1.1, step 3.1] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-periodic-fixed-n-limit",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Periodic fixed-particle canonical limit",
        "statement": "Assume the superstable regular interaction of [[thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure]], $\\beta>0$, and an interior attainable density $\\rho>0$. For integers $N_L$ with $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho$, let $Z_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,N_L;\\beta)$ be the $1/N_L!$-normalized positional partition on the periodized cube, with the image convention of [[def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction]]. Then\n$$L^{-d}\\log Z_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,N_L;\\beta)\\longrightarrow F_\\beta(\\rho),$$\nthe same rate as the free cube. Let $C^{\\mathrm{per}}_{L,N_L}$ be the periodic exact-$N_L$ canonical law and $R_{L,\\omega}$ its periodized empirical field from [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]]. For any finite supporting activity $z>0$ of $F_\\beta$ at $\\rho$, these empirical fields concentrate in every open $\\tau_L$ neighborhood of the minimizer set of $I+\\beta\\Phi-\\rho\\log z$. A singleton minimizer gives convergence in probability in $\\tau_L$.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap",
          "lem-tdmath-superstable-cell-energy-bound",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-grand-duality",
          "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
          "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
          "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density",
          "lem-tdmath-canonical-empirical-field-conditioning",
          "thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integrable-tail-diagonal-gap": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-superstable-cell-energy-bound": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-grand-duality": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-canonical-empirical-field-conditioning": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Lemma 4.3 and Sections 4–5, printed pp.181–187 / PDF pp.11–17: periodic/free energy comparison and local limits."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 1–3, printed pp.131–145 / PDF pp.4–18: lattice-tail energy control."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The assumptions and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 For the upper bound, $e^{\\alpha N_L}Z_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,N_L;\\beta)\\leq\\Xi_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,e^\\alpha,\\beta)$ for every real $\\alpha$. Divide logarithms by $L^d$, use the free/periodic grand limit [F6], and then take the infimum over $\\alpha$. The canonical-grand duality [F5] gives $\\limsup L^{-d}\\log Z_{\\mathrm{per}}\\leq F_\\beta(\\rho)$. [F4, F5, F6, algebra]\n\n1.2 Fix corridor width $k$ larger than the tail cutoff. Restrict the $N_L$ particles to the inner cube of side $L-k$, leaving the corridor empty. The exact-$N_L$ density is still $\\rho$. The diagonal-gap supplier [F2] gives the vanishing regular-tail cutoff used here. By [L1], the periodized energy differs from the free inner-cube energy by at most $c_d\\delta_kT_{L-k}$. The cell bound [F3] implies $T_{L-k}\\leq A^{-1}(H_{L-k}+BN_L)$. Consequently $$ Z_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,N_L;\\beta)\\geq e^{-\\beta c_d\\delta_k BN_L/A}\\, Z(L-k,N_L;\\beta(1+c_d\\delta_k/A)). $$ The canonical theorem [F4] applies at the perturbed inverse temperature, giving the corresponding lower rate. [F1, F2, F3, F4, L1, algebra]\n\n2.1 At fixed interior $\\rho$, $F_\\beta(\\rho)$ is finite and convex as a function of $\\beta>0$, by Hölder’s inequality [F8], since each finite-volume log partition is convex and the pointwise limit is finite. A finite convex function is continuous on the open interval: on a compact subinterval, its secant slopes are bounded by the slopes to fixed exterior endpoints. Let $L\\to\\infty$ in step 1.2 and then $k\\to\\infty$, so $\\delta_k\\to0$; continuity yields $\\liminf\\geq F_\\beta(\\rho)$. [F4, F8, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 Step 2.1 established the periodic exact-$N_L$ rate $F_\\beta(\\rho)$, and [F5] supplies a finite supporting activity. The grand rate in [F6] and the periodized empirical field in [F9] satisfy the hypotheses of [F7] with periodic boundary condition. Hence the periodic empirical fields concentrate near the minimizer set, and concentrate at its singleton when it is unique. [F5, F6, F7, F9, step 2.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-controlled-shell-three-ensemble-equivalence",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Controlled shrinking energy-density shell and three-ensemble equivalence",
        "statement": "Assume the superstable regular model of [[thm-tdmath-periodic-fixed-n-limit]], an interior density $N_L/L^d\\to\\rho$, and differentiability of $\\beta\\mapsto F_\\beta(\\rho)$ at $\\beta>0$. Put $e=-\\partial_\\beta F_\\beta(\\rho)$. Suppose the grand variational minimizer at a supporting activity $z>0$ is a singleton $P_*$. Let $R_{L,\\omega}$ be the periodized empirical field of [[def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field]]. There exists a specified sequence $\\delta_L\\downarrow0$ such that the uniform positional shell law\n$$Q_{\\Lambda_L}\\!\\left(\\,\\cdot\\ \\middle|\\ N=N_L,\\ \\left|H_{L,\\mathrm{per}}/L^d-e\\right|\\leq\\delta_L\\right)$$\nis eventually defined, and its empirical field converges in probability in $\\tau_L$ to $P_*$. The free and periodic exact-$N$ canonical empirical fields and the matching periodic grand-canonical empirical field converge in probability to the same limit. The energy width $2\\delta_LL^d=o(L^d)$; no arbitrary prescribed width or zero-width surface measure is asserted.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
          "thm-tdmath-fixed-n-local-ensemble-equivalence",
          "thm-tdmath-periodic-fixed-n-limit",
          "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field",
          "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-fixed-n-local-ensemble-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-periodic-fixed-n-limit": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-periodized-empirical-field-stationary-density": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 4–5, printed pp.181–187 / PDF pp.11–17: energy concentration and variational limits."
            },
            {
              "title": "D. Ruelle, Superstable Interactions in Classical Statistical Mechanics",
              "url": "https://www.ihes.fr/~ruelle/PUBLICATIONS/%5B28%5D.pdf",
              "locator": "Sections 1–3, printed pp.131–145 / PDF pp.4–18: cell coercivity and tail controls used for local compactness."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 9,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The assumptions and suppliers in Facts & Assumptions.\n\n1.1 Let $C_L$ be the periodic exact-$N_L$ canonical law. For $|t|<\\beta$, $$ E_{C_L}e^{tH_{\\mathrm{per}}} =Z_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,N_L;\\beta-t)/Z_{\\mathrm{per}}(L,N_L;\\beta). $$ By [F1], the normalized log moment generating function converges to $F_{\\beta-t}(\\rho)-F_\\beta(\\rho)$, whose derivative at zero is $e$. For any fixed $\\epsilon>0$, choose small positive and negative $t$ so the Chernoff exponents for $H/L^d\\geq e+\\epsilon$ and $H/L^d\\leq e-\\epsilon$ are strictly negative. Hence the canonical probability outside the fixed $\\epsilon$-window tends to zero. [F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Choose increasing thresholds $L_k$ so that for every $L\\geq L_k$ the probability outside the $1/k$ window is at most $1/k$. Set $k(L)=\\max\\{k:L_k\\leq L\\}$ and $\\delta_L=1/k(L)$. Then $\\delta_L\\downarrow0$ and the canonical probability of the selected shell tends to one. Positivity of the canonical weight on the shell implies positive Poisson reference measure, so the shell law is defined eventually. [step 1.1, choose]\n\n3.1 Let $U$ be any open $\\tau_L$ neighborhood of $P_*$ and let $S_L$ be the selected shell event. On $S_L$, the periodic canonical density relative to the uniform positional law lies between $e^{-\\beta L^d(e+\\delta_L)}$ and $e^{-\\beta L^d(e-\\delta_L)}$. Hence\n$$Q_{\\Lambda_L}(R_{L,\\omega}\\notin U\\mid S_L)\\leq\\frac{e^{2\\beta\\delta_L L^d}}{C_L^{\\mathrm{per}}(S_L)}\\,C_L^{\\mathrm{per}}(R_{L,\\omega}\\notin U).$$\nBy step 2.1, $C_L^{\\mathrm{per}}(S_L)\\to1$. By [F1], the last probability has a strictly negative exponential rate. Since $\\delta_L\\to0$, the right side tends to zero. Thus the uniform shell empirical field converges in probability to $P_*$. [F1, F4, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The free exact-$N$ empirical field converges in probability by [F2]. The periodic grand empirical fields obey the good LDP [L1]; its unique zero is $P_*$ by [F3], so the probability outside each neighborhood of $P_*$ tends to zero. Together with steps 2.1 and 3.1, this proves the stated three-ensemble conclusion. [F2, F3, F4, L1, step 3.1] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "order": 298,
    "id": "td-math-interacting-ensemble-geometry-examples",
