      "td-math-interacting-ensemble-geometry"
    ],
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-hard-core-zero-beta",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A hard core remains at zero inverse temperature",
        "statement": "In one spatial dimension, let $\\phi(x)=+\\infty$ for $|x|<a$ and $0$ otherwise, with $a>0$. On an interval $[0,L]$, define the beta-zero weight by retaining the forbidden-pair exclusion as in [[thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit]]. If $L>a$, the two-particle canonical partition is\n$$Z(L,2;0)=\\frac{(L-a)^2}{2},$$\nwhile the unrestricted ideal-gas value is $L^2/2$. Thus the retained hard-core beta-zero ensemble is not the Poisson ideal gas.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Section 2, printed pp.172–174 / PDF pp.2–4: finite-volume Boltzmann weights assign zero weight to forbidden infinite-energy configurations for positive inverse temperature. The retained-admissibility zero-parameter convention is supplied by [[thm-tdmath-retained-hard-zero-beta-limit]], with the finite calculation proved below; Theorem 4 on printed p.177 allows a nonnegative multiplier; this finite retained-exclusion calculation is proved locally and does not invoke a positive-temperature LDP at zero."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The one-dimensional hard-rod potential and the stated interval.\n\n1.1 On the ordered region $x_1<x_2$, allowed pairs satisfy $x_2\\geq x_1+a$, so their volume is $\\int_0^{L-a}(L-x_1-a)\\,dx_1=(L-a)^2/2$. The coordinate-swap map preserves product Lebesgue measure, so the region $x_2<x_1$ has the same volume; the diagonal has zero product measure. Thus the full labeled allowed volume is $(L-a)^2$, and division by $2!$ gives $Z(L,2;0)=(L-a)^2/2$. [F1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]\n\n2.1 Since $0<a<L$, $(L-a)^2/2<L^2/2$, the unrestricted ideal-gas two-particle canonical term. The strict deficit is caused by the forbidden separation band and remains at beta zero by [F2]. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "cex-tdmath-bulk-field-pressure",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "A volume-sized external field changes ideal-gas pressure",
        "statement": "At fixed activity $z>0$ and inverse temperature $\\beta>0$, adding any spatially constant one-particle external energy throughout a continuum ideal-gas region leaves the thermodynamic-limit log grand partition sum per volume unchanged, as would a negligible boundary perturbation.",
        "deps": [
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "thm-exponential-addition-formula"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-exponential-addition-formula": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "D. Dereudre, Introduction to the theory of Gibbs point processes",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.08105",
              "locator": "Section 1.3, PDF pp.10–12: finite-volume grand-canonical normalization; the calculation is derived directly here."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-close-packing-endpoint",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "The close-packing endpoint can have zero canonical weight",
        "statement": "In one dimension, let the pair potential be infinite when the separation is less than $a>0$, and zero otherwise. For $N\\geq1$ labeled rods in $[0,L]$, with canonical positional normalization $1/N!$, write $S=L-(N-1)a$. If $S>0$, translating the ordered coordinates by $y_i=x_i-(i-1)a$ gives\n$$Z(L,N;\\beta)=\\frac{S^N}{N!}=\\frac{(L-(N-1)a)^N}{N!},\\qquad \\beta>0.$$\nIf $S=0$, the allowed configuration set has zero Lebesgue measure and $Z(L,N;\\beta)=0$. Along $N\\to\\infty$ with $L=(N-1)a$, the density $N/L\\to1/a$ while every finite-volume partition is zero. Thus the thermodynamic close-packing endpoint is approached by a sequence of zero-weight finite systems; it is not realized by a positive canonical partition. The interior canonical limit theorem makes no assertion at that endpoint.",
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial",
          "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-superstable-regular-interaction": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-canonical-interior-density-limit": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-measure-of-a-box-of-every-kind": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-factorial-and-falling-factorial": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "D. Dereudre, Introduction to the theory of Gibbs point processes",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1701.08105",
              "locator": "Section 1.3, PDF pp.10–12: canonical positional normalization; the one-dimensional hard-rod integral is calculated here."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** $N\\geq1$ and $L\\geq(N-1)a$.\n\n1.1 Order the coordinates $0\\leq x_1<\\cdots<x_N\\leq L$. The exclusion constraints are $x_{i+1}-x_i\\geq a$. Apply the coordinatewise translation $x_i\\mapsto y_i=x_i-(i-1)a$; each one-coordinate translation preserves Lebesgue measure [F5], so product measure is preserved. The ordered region becomes $0\\leq y_1\\leq\\cdots\\leq y_N\\leq S$, where $S=L-(N-1)a$. The cube $[0,S]^N$ splits, up to equality sets of zero product measure, into $N!$ coordinate-order regions. Product-measure uniqueness makes these permutation chambers equal in measure, so the ordered region has volume $S^N/N!$. The full labeled allowed volume is $N!$ times this, and canonical division by $N!$ gives $Z(L,N;\\beta)=S^N/N!$. [F1, F3, F4, F5, algebra]\n\n2.1 If $L=(N-1)a$, then $S=0$ and the transformed region is a null set; its integral is zero. If $L>(N-1)a$, then $S>0$ and the ordered region has positive product volume. Thus the maximal-density endpoint does not carry a positive canonical partition at exact close packing; the interior-only theorem [F2] makes no contrary assertion. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-tdmath-nonunique-variational-minimizers",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Nonunique minimizers allow distinct local accumulation limits",
        "statement": "Suppose $J(P)=I(P)+\\beta\\Phi(P)-\\rho(P)\\log z$ has two distinct stationary minimizers $P_0\\ne P_1$ with equal finite value $m$. Every finite mixture $P_\\theta=\\theta P_0+(1-\\theta)P_1$, $0\\leq\\theta\\leq1$, also minimizes $J$. The sequence of stationary laws alternating between $P_0$ and $P_1$ has two distinct local accumulation points and does not converge. This conditional mathematical example shows why compactness alone yields minimizer-set accumulation; it does not assert a finite-volume ensemble realizes the sequence or that a particular continuum potential exhibits coexistence.",
        "deps": [
          "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure",
          "thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules",
          "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-tdmath-continuum-variational-pressure": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-specific-entropy-density": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-local-tame-topology": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "H.-O. Georgii, Large deviations and the equivalence of ensembles for Gibbsian particle systems with superstable interaction",
              "url": "https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~georgii/papers/LDPsuperstable.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorems 1–2, printed pp.178–181 / PDF pp.8–11: stationary variational minimizer set; this example is an abstract consequence, not an asserted phase-transition model."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "proof": "**Given:** The assumptions above.\n\n1.1 Let $P,R$ be stationary laws of finite specific entropy and let $P_\\theta=\\theta P+(1-\\theta)R$. For a bounded cube $G$, the binary-label chain rule [F3] gives $$ \\theta D(P_G\\Vert Q_G)+(1-\\theta)D(R_G\\Vert Q_G) =D((P_\\theta)_G\\Vert Q_G)+I(S;X_G), $$ where $S$ is the mixture label and $X_G$ the observed configuration. Since $0\\leq I(S;X_G)\\leq H(S)<\\infty$, divide by $|G|$ and take the cube limit [F2]; $H(S)/|G|\\to0$, so $I(P_\\theta)=\\theta I(P)+(1-\\theta)I(R)$. The expectation defining $\\rho$ is affine. For finite energy densities, $E_{P_\\theta}H_G=\\theta E_PH_G+(1-\\theta)E_RH_G$ for every $G$; dividing by volume and taking limits gives affinity of $\\Phi$. Thus $J$ is affine on finite mixtures of finite-$J$ laws. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 Applying step 1.1 to $P_0,P_1$ gives $J(P_\\theta)=\\theta J(P_0)+(1-\\theta)J(P_1)=m$, so every mixture is a minimizer. Put $P_{2n}=P_0$ and $P_{2n+1}=P_1$. The even and odd subsequences converge locally to $P_0$ and $P_1$. Since distinct laws differ on a bounded-region measurable event, its indicator is a bounded local test by [F4] with different expectations; the full sequence therefore has no $\\tau_L$ limit. Both accumulation points are minimizers. [given, F4, step 1.1, algebra] ∎\n",
        "library": "mathematics"
      }
    ]
  }
]
